{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And he took curd and milk and the calf.</b> And he did not bring bread, and the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded this: our mother Sarah separated dough as challah, and our father Abraham ate non-sacred food in purity, and therefore he did not bring the bread." "<b>And you should ask:</b> if Abraham ate non-sacred food in purity, this would not be an obligation upon the rest of people, and perhaps the intent of the Sages is that he did not wish to feed them from what he was not accustomed to eat himself. But the correct interpretation is that this was because of the prohibition of leaven, for since Abraham ate non-sacred food in purity she withdrew her hands from the dough, and Abraham was concerned about the prohibition of leaven, for it was the season of Passover — and this is why it says here ״עגות״ — cakes — ״לושי ועשי עגות״ — knead and make cakes. And in the matter of Lot it mentions מצות — unleavened cakes — in order to draw a correspondence between the two: cakes of unleavened bread. And behold, the entire Torah is allusions, and what is lacking here is revealed here. And so you will find the like of these matters in many places throughout the Torah:" "<b>And they ate.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): those fit for eating — this is Ishmael and the rest of the members of Abraham's household — and similarly (Isaiah 8): ״והיה כנור ונבל תוף וחליל ויין משתיהם״ — the lyre and harp, timbrel and flute and wine, are their drink; the timbrel and the flute are not fit for drinking with the wine, but rather the timbrel and flute are fit for playing and the wine for drinking. And by way of the Midrash: ״ויאכלו״ — they appeared as though they ate, for the flesh would disappear, one piece after another. And this is in the manner of what is written (Judges 6:21): ״וישלח מלאך ה׳ את קצה המשענת אשר בידו ויגע בבשר ובמצות ותעל האש מן הצור ותאכל״ — a thing that disappears and is consumed is called אכילה, from the language of (Deuteronomy 32:42) ״וחרבי תאכל בשר,״ and (Deuteronomy 32:30 — in our texts 7:1) ״ואכלת את כל העמים.״" "<b>Know that</b> the entire passage, some interpret it as a prophetic vision, and they seek to compel the matter with the argument that a person cannot apprehend seeing angels with the eye of the flesh except in a vision. And according to this understanding that reason grasps, Sarah did not knead cakes and Abraham did not prepare the calf, but it was in a prophetic vision; and we would also have to say this regarding Lot, that he rose to the level of the rank of prophecy, and also the men of Sodom, regarding whom Scripture testifies that they were ״רעים וחטאים״ — evil and sinful — and how is it possible that Lot would ascend to the level of prophecy, when the Holy One, blessed be He, withheld speech from Abraham the entire time that Lot was with him, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 13:14): ״וה׳ אמר אל אברם אחרי הפרד לוט מעמו.״ But the essence of this passage in its entirety is according to the plain sense of the verses, and the matters of Sarah and Abraham and Lot and the men of Sodom — all is literally according to its plain sense, not a prophetic vision. And if your heart hesitates — how can angels be apprehended by the eyes of the flesh except in a vision? This is true and established, for the faculties of perception cannot possibly apprehend intelligibles through their senses. And this passage cannot be taught to any person of understanding except by way of the Kabbalah. It is that these angels are called by the name אנשים — men: the intent in them concerns a created honor among the angels, and the masters of truth call it a garment (מלבוש), for it is a garment for three supernal ranks called אדם — man — which clothe themselves in this measure called garment, and since the destruction of the Temple this measure, which is the garment, fell." "<b>And you already know</b> that the soul is called אדם — man — not the body, for the body is nothing but the garment of the soul. And so Scripture testifies (Job 10:11): ״עור ובשר תלבישני״ — skin and flesh You clothe me with — explaining that the body is the garment and the soul, which is the man, clothes itself in it. And that they are called אנשים in this passage — and the same applies in the other places throughout all the verses where angels are called by the name אנשים — the intent in them concerns this garment. And that the verses call them by the name אנשים is because it is perceptible to the eyes of the body, to those who are pure of soul. And this is the substance of the passage, that it calls them sometimes אנשים and sometimes מלאכים: מלאכים because they were literally angels; אנשים because they are the angels called garment. Make your ear like a funnel and arouse yourself to understand this matter." "<b>And further.</b> Know that there is a secret in the matter of this parashah by another path, and it is that they were actual men and actual angels. And consider well that Scripture called the angels by the name 'men' in the matter of Abraham, while in the matter of Lot it called them 'angels.' And the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded: here they are called 'men' and there they are called 'angels' — here they are called 'men' when the Shekhinah was upon them, and there they are called 'angels' when the Shekhinah departed and they put on the attribute of angels. This is what is written there: ״וילך ה׳״ — 'and YHWH went,' and it is written: ״ובאו שני המלאכים סדומה״ — 'and the two angels came to Sodom.'" "<b>And you should reflect</b> that if Scripture had called them only 'angels' in the matter of Lot, the reason of this midrash would suffice. But you will find that Scripture called them in the matter of Lot once 'angels' and once 'men': as it is written ״ובאו שני המלאכים סדומה״, and it is written ״וישלחו האנשים את ידם ויביאו את לוט וגו׳״, and it is written ״ויאיצו המלאכים״, and immediately after it: ״ויחזיקו האנשים״. From all this you should reflect that this is a strong proof that they were actual men and actual angels, and they did everything they did with the knowledge of the angels." "<b>And from now on</b> the true verses will come as their plain sense, both in the name 'men' and in the name 'angels,' and both the revealed and the hidden in them are true. And this is the matter of speech in the serpent and in the donkey, and the discerning will understand. And the intention in this is what the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said in Bava Metzia, chapter 'One who hires laborers' (Bava Metzia 85a): Elijah used to frequent the academy of Rabbi. One day, the New Moon day, he was delayed and did not come. He said to him: why was the master delayed? He said to him: I was occupied with Abraham, and I washed his hands and he prayed and I escorted him; and likewise with Isaac and likewise with Jacob. And I stood them all together, thinking to strengthen them with mercy and bring the Messiah before his time. He said to him: is there anything like them in this world? He said to him: there is Rabbi Ḥiyya and his sons. Rabbi decreed a fast and ordained Rabbi Ḥiyya and his sons. He said 'Who makes the wind blow' — the wind came; he said 'Who makes the rain fall' — the rain came. When he was about to say 'Who revives the dead,' the world trembled. They said in heaven: who revealed secrets in the world? Elijah. They brought Elijah and struck him with sixty lashes of fire. He went and showed himself like a bear of fire, entered among them and scattered them, and they did not say 'Who revives the dead.' And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:42.252126310Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}